00:31:43,120 --> 00:31:49,919
over 100 m, we can solve for I2.

834
00:31:45,600 --> 00:31:53,840
Rearranging, we get I2 = 10 ^ of -3 *

835
00:31:49,919 --> 00:31:56,320
100 m, which equals 0.1 mwatt. So after

836
00:31:53,840 --> 00:31:59,039
losing 30 dB, the intensity is reduced

837
00:31:56,320 --> 00:32:01,039
by a factor of 1,000.

838
00:31:59,039 --> 00:32:03,120
Now let's look at the second example.

839
00:32:01,039 --> 00:32:04,640
The half value thickness. This

840
00:32:03,120 --> 00:32:07,919
represents the depth at which the

841
00:32:04,640 --> 00:32:11,360
ultrasound intensity drops by 50%. That

842
00:32:07,919 --> 00:32:13,440
means I2 over I1= 0.5.

843
00:32:11,360 --> 00:32:17,919
Using the decibel formula again we get

844
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:19,919
10 log of 0.5= -3 dB. So when the

845
00:32:17,919 --> 00:32:23,120
intensity is reduced by half, it

846
00:32:19,919 --> 00:32:25,200
corresponds to a 3 decel change.

847
00:32:23,120 --> 00:32:27,519
This concept is very similar to what we

848
00:32:25,200 --> 00:32:30,240
use in X-ray or gammaray imaging where

849
00:32:27,519 --> 00:32:32,480
we also talk about a half value layer.

850
00:32:30,240 --> 00:32:35,440
It tells us how deep the wave can travel

851
00:32:32,480 --> 00:32:37,600
before its intensity is halved.

852
00:32:35,440 --> 00:32:39,760
In ultrasound, this is just a convenient

853
00:32:37,600 --> 00:32:42,159
way to express relative changes using

854
00:32:39,760 --> 00:32:44,320
logarithms. There's no physical constant

855
00:32:42,159 --> 00:32:46,880
behind it. It's simply a mathematical

856
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:48,799
convenience. The decibel scale helps us

857
00:32:46,880 --> 00:32:50,880
describe very large or very small

858
00:32:48,799 --> 00:32:53,840
intensity ratios in a practical and

859
00:32:50,880 --> 00:32:53,840
readable way.

860
00:32:55,679 --> 00:32:59,600
Okay, now we'll talk about acoustic

861
00:32:57,200 --> 00:33:01,279
impedance. This concept is actually not

862
00:32:59,600 --> 00:33:03,519
dramatically different from what we've

863
00:33:01,279 --> 00:33:04,799
already learned in electrical circuits.

864
00:33:03,519 --> 00:33:07,519
Remember when we studied circuit

865
00:33:04,799 --> 00:33:09,440
analysis,

866
00:33:07,519 --> 00:33:11,760
we defined resistance as the ratio

867
00:33:09,440 --> 00:33:14,000
between voltage and current. Later we

868
00:33:11,760 --> 00:33:16,320
introduced impetence which included the

869
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:18,559
effects of capacitance and inductance

870
00:33:16,320 --> 00:33:20,240
and again it was defined as voltage over

871
00:33:18,559 --> 00:33:22,320
current.

872
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:24,240
The only difference was that in general

873
00:33:22,320 --> 00:33:26,159
impedance can have a phase shift. So

874
00:33:24,240 --> 00:33:28,000
it's expressed as a complex number

875
00:33:26,159 --> 00:33:30,240
meaning that both voltage and current

876
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:31,679
can be complex or we can simply describe

877
00:33:30,240 --> 00:33:33,360
them in terms of their phase

878
00:33:31,679 --> 00:33:35,039
relationship.

879
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:37,279
Now think about this analogy in the

880
00:33:35,039 --> 00:33:39,919
context of ultrasound. In an electrical

881
00:33:37,279 --> 00:33:41,360
circuit voltage is the driving force the

882
00:33:39,919 --> 00:33:43,919
potential difference that pushes

883
00:33:41,360 --> 00:33:45,760
electrons through the conductor.

884
00:33:43,919 --> 00:33:48,320
In ultrasound the driving force is

885
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:50,080
pressure P. The mechanical wave you push

886
00:33:48,320 --> 00:33:52,799
into the medium. That's the pressure

887
00:33:50,080 --> 00:33:54,559
wave. So just as voltage drives electron

888
00:33:52,799 --> 00:33:57,679
motion, pressure drives the particle

889
00:33:54,559 --> 00:33:59,120
motion in the tissue. In an electrical

890
00:33:57,679 --> 00:34:01,440
circuit, current is the flow of

891
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:03,200
electrons. But in ultrasound, no

892
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:05,360
electrons are flowing. Instead, the

893
00:34:03,200 --> 00:34:07,360
medium's molecules, air molecules, or

894
00:34:05,360 --> 00:34:09,839
soft tissue molecules move back and

895
00:34:07,360 --> 00:34:11,760
forth along one direction.

896
00:34:09,839 --> 00:34:13,919
At any given location, they have an

897
00:34:11,760 --> 00:34:16,399
instantaneous velocity, which we call

898
00:34:13,919 --> 00:34:18,480
uz. So at each point in space, the

899
00:34:16,399 --> 00:34:21,040
particles have an instantaneous velocity

900
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:23,359
that's changing with time.

901
00:34:21,040 --> 00:34:25,359
Both P and U keep changing with time.

902
00:34:23,359 --> 00:34:28,000
But when we solve the wave equation, we

903
00:34:25,359 --> 00:34:30,000
find something very interesting.

904
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:32,079
It's beyond the scope of this lecture to

905
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:34,000
show the full derivation. But the result

906
00:34:32,079 --> 00:34:37,280
is that the ratio between pressure and

907
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:39,280
velocity P over U is constant at any

908
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:41,440
given location. Even though both

909
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:43,520
pressure and velocity oscillate as the

910
00:34:41,440 --> 00:34:45,679
wave propagates, their ratio remains

911
00:34:43,520 --> 00:34:48,079
constant in amplitude. Because this

912
00:34:45,679 --> 00:34:50,000
ratio stays constant, we can define it

913
00:34:48,079 --> 00:34:51,599
as a characteristic property of the

914
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:53,119
medium.

915
00:34:51,599 --> 00:34:56,079
This constant ratio is what we call

916
00:34:53,119 --> 00:34:58,079
acoustic impedance denoted by Z. If the

917
00:34:56,079 --> 00:35:00,240
ratio kept changing, it would depend on

918
00:34:58,079 --> 00:35:02,079
position or time and we couldn't define

919
00:35:00,240 --> 00:35:03,839
it as an intrinsic property of the

920
00:35:02,079 --> 00:35:05,200
material.

921
00:35:03,839 --> 00:35:06,800
But because it's stable and

922
00:35:05,200 --> 00:35:08,720
characteristic, it gives us something

923
00:35:06,800 --> 00:35:11,839
meaningful, a property that tells us how

924
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:13,440
the medium resists the passage of sound.

925
00:35:11,839 --> 00:35:15,440
Acoustic impedance is extremely

926
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:17,520
important in ultrasound imaging. When we

927
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:19,440
use an ultrasound transducer, we have to

928
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:21,440
make sure that the acoustic impedance of

929
00:35:19,440 --> 00:35:23,520
the transducer matches the acoustic

930
00:35:21,440 --> 00:35:26,160
impedance of the tissue we're imaging.

931
00:35:23,520 --> 00:35:28,079
For example, the abdomen.

932
00:35:26,160 --> 00:35:30,000
If there's a big mismatch between them,

933
00:35:28,079 --> 00:35:31,920
the energy cannot enter the body

934
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:34,560
effectively and you can't collect the

935
00:35:31,920 --> 00:35:36,480
echo signals efficiently. That's why we

936
00:35:34,560 --> 00:35:37,760
use ultrasound gel. It acts as a

937
00:35:36,480 --> 00:35:39,599
coupling layer that bridges the

938
00:35:37,760 --> 00:35:42,720
impedance gap between the probe and the

939
00:35:39,599 --> 00:35:44,800
skin. So to define it heristically,

940
00:35:42,720 --> 00:35:46,880
acoustic impedance is like voltage over

941
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:49,839
current in an electrical system. Here

942
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:52,000
it's pressure over particle velocity.

943
00:35:49,839 --> 00:35:53,839
We can show this mathematically though I

944
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:56,000
won't go through the full derivation.

945
00:35:53,839 --> 00:35:58,160
Now by solving the mechanical wave

946
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:00,160
equation we can prove that Z is constant

947
00:35:58,160 --> 00:36:02,480
and depends only on the material's

948
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:06,960
intrinsic properties specifically

949
00:36:02,480 --> 00:36:09,440
density RH and sound speed C.

950
00:36:06,960 --> 00:36:12,480
Mathematically we can write this as Z=

951
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:14,880
RO * C. And we know that the sound speed

952
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:17,760
C can be expressed as 1 over the square

953
00:36:14,880 --> 00:36:19,839
root of RH * kappa where kappa is the

954
00:36:17,760 --> 00:36:22,160
compressibility.

955
00:36:19,839 --> 00:36:25,200
So substituting that into the equation

956
00:36:22,160 --> 00:36:28,480
we get Z= RH over the square roo<unk> of

957
00:36:25,200 --> 00:36:31,520
RAO * kappa which simplifies to Z= the

958
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:33,440
square roo<unk> of IO over kappa.

959
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:35,200
This shows that acoustic impedance

960
00:36:33,440 --> 00:36:37,599
depends on both density and

961
00:36:35,200 --> 00:36:41,400
compressibility and it is a fundamental

962
00:36:37,599 --> 00:36:41,400
property of the material.

963
00:36:41,760 --> 00:36:46,720
Now once we have this equation Z equals

964
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:48,240
the square root of RHO over kappa we can

965
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:49,680
calculate or measure the acoustic

966
00:36:48,240 --> 00:36:52,640
impedance for different types of

967
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:54,240
biological tissues. Equation 3.8 and the

968
00:36:52,640 --> 00:36:57,200
table shown here give you typical

969
00:36:54,240 --> 00:36:59,119
values. If you look at the numbers you

970
00:36:57,200 --> 00:37:01,599
can get a feeling for how impedance and

971
00:36:59,119 --> 00:37:04,079
sound speed vary across materials. In

972
00:37:01,599 --> 00:37:06,480
air the sound speed is only about 330

973
00:37:04,079 --> 00:37:08,240
m/s because air is very light and

974
00:37:06,480 --> 00:37:10,400
compressible.

975
00:37:08,240 --> 00:37:12,640
In biological tissues like the kidney or

976
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:16,640
liver, the sound speed is much higher.

977
00:37:12,640 --> 00:37:18,400
So around 5,000 drum 60 to 1,70 m/s

978
00:37:16,640 --> 00:37:20,480
because these materials are denser and

979
00:37:18,400 --> 00:37:22,560
less compressible.

980
00:37:20,480 --> 00:37:24,640
Now if you ask where sound travels the

981
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:26,880
fastest inside the human body, the

982
00:37:24,640 --> 00:37:29,760
answer is bone because bone is the

983
00:37:26,880 --> 00:37:32,800
densest and stiffest material we have.

984
00:37:29,760 --> 00:37:35,280
The table shows a speed of about 3,500

985
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:37,839
m/s in bone, which is several times

986
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:39,680
faster than in soft tissue.

987
00:37:37,839 --> 00:37:41,920
These are just typical examples of

988
00:37:39,680 --> 00:37:43,839
tissue acoustic properties, but they are

989
00:37:41,920 --> 00:37:46,160
extremely important for understanding

990
00:37:43,839 --> 00:37:48,400
how ultrasound interacts with different

991
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:50,720
parts of the body.

992
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:52,800
The speed of sound, the density, and the

993
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:55,119
impedance all influence how much of the

994
00:37:52,800 --> 00:37:59,160
soundwave is transmitted, reflected, or

995
00:37:55,119 --> 00:37:59,160
absorbed at tissue boundaries.

996
00:38:00,640 --> 00:38:04,720
So next, let's talk about ultrasound

997
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:06,480
tissue interaction. Earlier we mentioned

998
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:08,880
how different imaging modalities

999
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:10,880
interact with biological tissues. For

1000
00:38:08,880 --> 00:38:12,480
example, X-ray and CT involve

1001
00:38:10,880 --> 00:38:15,440
attenuation and scattering while

1002
00:38:12,480 --> 00:38:17,920
gammaray imaging behaves similarly.

1003
00:38:15,440 --> 00:38:20,240
In MRI, we talk about T1 and T2

1004
00:38:17,920 --> 00:38:22,720
relaxation parameters to describe energy

1005
00:38:20,240 --> 00:38:25,119
interactions within tissues.

1006
00:38:22,720 --> 00:38:26,720
For ultrasound, the same basic idea

1007
00:38:25,119 --> 00:38:28,560
applies. It's all about energy

1008
00:38:26,720 --> 00:38:31,119
propagation and how that energy

1009
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:33,040
interacts with biological structures.

1010
00:38:31,119 --> 00:38:34,720
The first major effect we talk about is

1011
00:38:33,040 --> 00:38:36,880
reflection.

1012
00:38:34,720 --> 00:38:38,320
When a soundwave hits a boundary where

1013
00:38:36,880 --> 00:38:40,480
there is a difference in acoustic

1014
00:38:38,320 --> 00:38:42,880
impedance, for example, between air and

1015
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:45,119
tissue or between bone and tissue, some

1016
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:46,960
of the energy is reflected. That's what

1017
00:38:45,119 --> 00:38:49,119
creates the echoes we use to form

1018
00:38:46,960 --> 00:38:51,119
ultrasound images.

1019
00:38:49,119 --> 00:38:53,119
Reflection happens because of impedance

1020
00:38:51,119 --> 00:38:55,119
mismatch. If the impedance difference is

1021
00:38:53,119 --> 00:38:57,119
large, reflection is strong. If the

1022
00:38:55,119 --> 00:39:00,400
impedances are well matched, reflection

1023
00:38:57,119 --> 00:39:02,160
is weak and more energy passes through.

1024
00:39:00,400 --> 00:39:03,760
Now with X-rays and gamma rays,

1025
00:39:02,160 --> 00:39:05,599
reflection is not really an issue

1026
00:39:03,760 --> 00:39:07,680
because their wavelengths are extremely

1027
00:39:05,599 --> 00:39:09,680
short and the photons are so energetic

1028
00:39:07,680 --> 00:39:11,520
that they mostly pass straight through.

1029
00:39:09,680 --> 00:39:13,440
In ultrasound, however, the wavelength

1030
00:39:11,520 --> 00:39:15,200
is much larger. So reflection becomes

1031
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:17,359
significant.

1032
00:39:15,200 --> 00:39:19,040
So remember this simple rule, reflection

1033
00:39:17,359 --> 00:39:20,720
at a boundary means a change in

1034
00:39:19,040 --> 00:39:23,040
direction.

1035
00:39:20,720 --> 00:39:24,880
The second effect is scattering. When

1036
00:39:23,040 --> 00:39:26,960
ultrasound waves travel through tissue,

1037
00:39:24,880 --> 00:39:29,599
they encounter many small structures,

1038
00:39:26,960 --> 00:39:32,880
cells, organels, connective fibers that

1039
00:39:29,599 --> 00:39:34,800
scatter energy in different directions.

1040
00:39:32,880 --> 00:39:36,640
So instead of traveling as a narrow

1041
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:38,800
beam, the energy spreads out or

1042
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:41,280
diffuses. You can think of this like

1043
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:43,119
Compton scattering in X-rays. Energy is

1044
00:39:41,280 --> 00:39:45,280
diffused and the propagation direction

1045
00:39:43,119 --> 00:39:47,520
changes randomly.

1046
00:39:45,280 --> 00:39:50,320
The third effect is absorption, which

1047
00:39:47,520 --> 00:39:51,920
converts mechanical energy into heat. In

1048
00:39:50,320 --> 00:39:53,920
the case of X-rays, this corresponds to

1049
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:56,560
the photoelectric effect where photon

1050
00:39:53,920 --> 00:39:58,640
energy is absorbed completely.

1051
00:39:56,560 --> 00:40:00,880
For ultrasound, as the wave passes

1052
00:39:58,640 --> 00:40:02,880
through tissue, the molecules oscillate

1053
00:40:00,880 --> 00:40:04,800
back and forth and because of internal

1054
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:07,440
friction, some of the mechanical energy

1055
00:40:04,800 --> 00:40:09,359
is lost as heat. This leads to

1056
00:40:07,440 --> 00:40:12,000
attenuation, which is the overall

1057
00:40:09,359 --> 00:40:13,920
reduction in intensity. Attenuation

1058
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:15,839
happens because energy is both absorbed

1059
00:40:13,920 --> 00:40:18,079
and scattered away.

1060
00:40:15,839 --> 00:40:20,240
the wave's amplitude becomes smaller,

1061
00:40:18,079 --> 00:40:22,480
its pressure decreases, and the particle

1062
00:40:20,240 --> 00:40:25,920
displacements become weaker as it moves

1063
00:40:22,480 --> 00:40:27,599
deeper. If we look at figure 3.3, it

1064
00:40:25,920 --> 00:40:29,200
illustrates how the beam behaves when it

1065
00:40:27,599 --> 00:40:32,480
meets a boundary between two materials

1066
00:40:29,200 --> 00:40:34,480
with impedances Z1 and Z2. If the wave

1067
00:40:32,480 --> 00:40:36,480
hits perpendicularly, part of it is

1068
00:40:34,480 --> 00:40:38,000
reflected back and part is transmitted

1069
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:39,839
forward.

1070
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:41,920
In more general cases where the wave

1071
00:40:39,839 --> 00:40:44,160
hits the surface at an angle, we define

1072
00:40:41,920 --> 00:40:46,320
the incident angle theta I, the

1073
00:40:44,160 --> 00:40:49,359
reflected angle theta r, and the

1074
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:51,440
transmitted angle theta t.

1075
00:40:49,359 --> 00:40:53,200
The incident angle equals the reflected

1076
00:40:51,440 --> 00:40:54,720
angle, but the transmission angle can

1077
00:40:53,200 --> 00:40:56,400
differ depending on the material

1078
00:40:54,720 --> 00:40:58,319
properties.

1079
00:40:56,400 --> 00:41:00,480
The energy divides between reflection

1080
00:40:58,319 --> 00:41:03,440
and transmission. And the total energy

1081
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:05,440
always equals the incident energy. The

1082
00:41:03,440 --> 00:41:07,119
ratios of reflected to transmitted

1083
00:41:05,440 --> 00:41:09,040
energy can be expressed in terms of

1084
00:41:07,119 --> 00:41:10,960
pressure amplitude and when you square

1085
00:41:09,040 --> 00:41:13,359
those amplitudes you get the intensity

1086
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:15,599
ratios. These ratios depend on the

1087
00:41:13,359 --> 00:41:17,920
acoustic impedance values of the two

1088
00:41:15,599 --> 00:41:20,240
materials Z1 and Z2 as well as the

1089
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:23,040
angles of incidence and transmission. If

1090
00:41:20,240 --> 00:41:24,960
Z1 equals Z2, meaning the materials are

1091
00:41:23,040 --> 00:41:26,880
identical, there will be no reflection

1092
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:29,040
at all and all the energy will pass

1093
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:31,040
straight through. But if the impedances

1094
00:41:29,040 --> 00:41:32,800
are very different, for example, if one

1095
00:41:31,040 --> 00:41:34,960
of them is close to zero, then the

1096
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:36,880
reflection coefficient becomes one.

1097
00:41:34,960 --> 00:41:39,440
Meaning all the energy is reflected

1098
00:41:36,880 --> 00:41:41,760
back. That's why impedance matching is

1099
00:41:39,440 --> 00:41:43,680
so important in ultrasound imaging. It

1100
00:41:41,760 --> 00:41:45,359
ensures that most of the sound energy

1101
00:41:43,680 --> 00:41:47,760
enters the body instead of being

1102
00:41:45,359 --> 00:41:49,839
reflected away at the surface. So in

1103
00:41:47,760 --> 00:41:52,319
summary, ultrasound tissue interaction

